 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Whether you should wear a mask outside in Singapore depends on your health status and how long you plan to be outdoors, due to the current **haze situation** affecting the island. \n\nAir quality is currently in the **unhealthy to very unhealthy** range (with 24-hour PSI readings hovering well above normal levels). \n\nThe Ministry of Health (MOH) and National Environment Agency (NEA) guidelines advise the following regarding outdoor masks and activity:\n\n* **For Healthy Individuals:** \n  * If you are just doing short outdoor commutes (like walking to public transport or running a quick errand), a mask is generally not strictly required, but you should avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion.\n  * If you must exercise or spend extended periods outside, check the live **1-hour PM2.5 readings** on the `myENV` app or `haze.gov.sg`. If PM2.5 levels are in the high band, you should use a well-fitting **N95 mask** if you cannot avoid being out.\n* **For Vulnerable Groups (Elderly, Pregnant Women, Children):** \n  * You should **minimize or avoid outdoor activities**. \n  * If you must go outside when PM2.5 levels are high, it is recommended that you **wear an N95 mask** to reduce exposure to fine particles.\n* **For Persons with Chronic Heart or Lung Disease:** \n  * You should **avoid outdoor activities altogether**. If you absolutely must venture out, wear an N95 mask.\n\n**Quick Tip:** Check the real-time 1-hour PM2.5 readings on your phone right before you step out to make the best decision for your specific location and activity level.",
